    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-102548",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4d059c56-ae7f-5fb6-8bc6-2ce97bb7f876",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Roots of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index",
      "problem": "Find the root of $f(x)=-3\\sqrt[6]{2x-5}-3.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt[6]{2x-5}-3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt[6]{2x-5}-3 & =0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[6]{2x-5} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{2x-5} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no root.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x=2",
