    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-17247",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-3(2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(2{e}^{x}-6x)\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2{e}^{0}-6\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
