  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932637",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-160028",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b346e7e1-b641-5a97-9677-84a0ff6ab2dc",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Missing Values in a Data Set Given the Mean",
      "problem": "Given that the mean of the following data set is $11,$ find the value of $c:$ \n\n$$\n(c-3),\\,6,\\,3,\\,c,\\,10,\\,20\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =(c-3)+6+3+c+10+20 \\\\  & =36+2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (36+2c) \\\\  & =\\frac{36+2c}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =\\frac{36+2c}{6} \\\\ 66 & =36+2c \\\\ 30 & =2c \\\\ c & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
