    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-124816",
      "knowledge_point_id": "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/3687e362fea73defca43332ee70f232282ad1eded9dc14cbbc045de4324067f0.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{6}{7}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{6}{7})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{36}{49}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{36}{49} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{49} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $x$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
